  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Instantaneous Power of a Signal",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute $p(t)=|x(t)|^2$ from a continuous-time signal $x(t)$ and interpret it as the pointwise signal-size quantity that underlies energy and average-power calculations.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Instantaneous Power of a Signal",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Instantaneous Power of a Signal",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Instantaneous power is the pointwise signal-size quantity attached to a continuous-time signal $x(t)$. It tells you how large the signal is at a single time, so the rule is local in time rather than over an interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(t)=|x(t)|^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To compute it, take the magnitude of $x(t)$ at that same time and square it. The squaring removes sign, so a real sample like $x(t_0)=3$ gives the same power as any other value with magnitude $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(t_0)=|x(t_0)|^2=|3|^2=9",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So if $x(t_0)=3$, the instantaneous power at that time is $9$. The same step works at every time: substitute the current signal value, square its magnitude, and keep the result as a new pointwise quantity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That pointwise quantity is the one that will later be accumulated into energy and averaged into average power. For now, keep the rule fixed: $p(t)$ is the squared magnitude of $x(t)$ at the same time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Instantaneous Power of a Signal",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing Instantaneous Power at a Single Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(2)=-4$, what is $p(2)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a single known signal value at one time: $x(2)=-4$. Use the definition from the tutorial, $p(t)=|x(t)|^2$, and substitute the given time into the signal before doing anything else.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(2)=|x(2)|^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now replace $x(2)$ with the given value and take its magnitude. The minus sign disappears inside the absolute value, so the quantity inside the square is $4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(2)=|-4|^2=4^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Square that magnitude to get $16$. Instantaneous power is pointwise signal size, so the final answer is positive even though the original signal value was negative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So for $x(2)=-4$, the instantaneous power is $p(2)=16$. The same substitution pattern will work whenever a question gives one time and one signal value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Instantaneous Power of a Signal",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Computing an Instantaneous Power Signal from a Formula",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=2t-1$, find the instantaneous power signal $p(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a formula for $x(t)$, so the goal is to turn that whole signal into a power signal using the same rule from the previous steps: $p(t)=|x(t)|^2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(t)=|x(t)|^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $x(t)=2t-1$ into the definition. Because $2t-1$ is real-valued, the magnitude is just the absolute value of the expression, so the power signal becomes $|2t-1|^2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(t)=|2t-1|^2=(2t-1)^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The absolute value disappears after squaring, so the simplified power signal is $p(t)=(2t-1)^2$. The result is a full function of $t$, not just one number, because the input was a formula for the whole signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the instantaneous power signal is $p(t)=(2t-1)^2$. The same substitution pattern will work for any formula you are given, as long as you keep the magnitude square around the signal first.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Instantaneous Power of a Signal",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Computing Instantaneous Power from Piecewise Signal Values",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=4$ for $0 \\le t < 1$, $x(t)=-1$ for $1 \\le t < 2$, and $x(t)=0$ otherwise, find the instantaneous power signal $p(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a piecewise signal, so apply the same rule on each interval: $p(t)=|x(t)|^2$. Keep the interval boundaries unchanged and only square the signal values.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(t)=|x(t)|^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "On $0 \\le t < 1$, the value is $4$, so $p(t)=|4|^2=16$. On $1 \\le t < 2$, the value is $-1$, so $p(t)=|-1|^2=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(t)=\\begin{cases}16, & 0 \\le t < 1\\\\1, & 1 \\le t < 2\\\\0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Outside the listed intervals, $x(t)=0$, so $p(t)=0$. The negative middle piece still gives positive power because the magnitude is squared.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the instantaneous power signal is $p(t)=16$ on $0 \\le t < 1$, $p(t)=1$ on $1 \\le t < 2$, and $p(t)=0$ otherwise.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Instantaneous Power of a Signal",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Zero and Larger Instantaneous Power Values",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $p(t_1)=0$ and $p(t_2)=9$, what can you conclude about $x(t_1)$ and the magnitude of $x(t_2)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a pair of power values, so read each one through the rule $p(t)=|x(t)|^2$ rather than treating power as a signed signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "p(t)=|x(t)|^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If $p(t_1)=0$, then $|x(t_1)|^2=0$, so $x(t_1)=0$. If $p(t_2)=9$, then $|x(t_2)|^2=9$, so $|x(t_2)|=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t_1)=0,\\qquad |x(t_2)|=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The sign of $x(t_2)$ is not determined by power alone, but its magnitude is larger than at $t_1$ because $3>0$. Zero power means zero signal value, and larger power means larger magnitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
